{
  "dom_id": "step-t6185",
  "name": "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases",
  "problem": "Let's consider the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/09b25bca42f62b6567cecf4b14987113003ccea6dd869a569be75af4fa1781c9.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve at this point. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/001914b359c9c95f6a8fceb655a5233242065eb4231765bcfc0d652ebbf4eed8.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines become steeper and steeper as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/40de3bbd7f8422ff4ab4af6091497579f8031b8a43296e466f8c23ed4a121097.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* as we approach $C,$ it would appear that these tangent lines become vertical. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes vertical like this, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at $C.$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-t6185\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n               \n                <div class=\"tutorial\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases</span></div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"t6185\">?</div>\n                    </div>                    \n    \n                    <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\n                            transition: none;\n           